// Distributed schedule for example 03-reduction — STRETCH.
//
// Status at TASK-0022: this schedule parses, lowers, and links — but
// the e2e gate is expected to fail because TASK-0122 only emits
// whole-symbol transfers, not per-partition slices. The distributed
// placement `place accumulate on { w0, w1, w2, w3 }` triggers the
// pthreads-sync backend's `UnsupportedFeature` rejection
// (`distributed_placement_is_rejected` test in the backend). Filed
// as a follow-up; when iteration-space partitioning lands
// (TASK-0117) AND per-tile transfer codegen lands (TASK-0126), this
// schedule can drive end-to-end.
//
// The shape this aims at:
//   host    — owns I/O (load_input / save_output) and the phase-2
//             tree combine (cheap, sequential).
//   w0..w3  — each compute worker owns one partition of `a` and
//             produces its `partials[w]`. The compiler partitions
//             the outer `w` loop across the four workers.
//
// Cross-worker edges this schedule declares:
//   a        host -> { w0..w3 }   (host loads, workers consume)
//   partials { w0..w3 } -> host   (workers produce, host combines)
//
// All `sync` (the pthreads-sync backend's only transfer mode).
// `partition=workers` is the partition policy for the outer loop —
// each worker owns a contiguous `PARTITION_SIZE`-wide slice
// (`rows` and `blocks2d` are the alternatives in v2's grammar; both
// imply a 2D iteration nest with a row/block structure, neither
// matches a 1D partition-axis loop). The 2D shape
// `a : i32[NUM_WORKERS][PARTITION_SIZE]` already exposes the
// partition as the outer axis; `partition=workers` on `loop w`
// names the policy explicitly.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // I/O on host.
    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;

    // Phase 1 — distributed across compute workers.
    place accumulate on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Phase 2 — cheap, sequential, on host.
    place combine on host;

    // Outer loop is the partition axis; one slice per compute worker.
    loop w : partition=workers;

    // Cross-worker data — one transfer per data symbol that crosses.
    transfer a        : sync;
    transfer partials : sync;
}
